---
description: General rules to be followed at ALL times
alwaysApply: true
---
- Use as little code as possible - make it extremely simple with as few lines as possible to complete the task. Stay in scope. DO NOT overcomplicate anything if fewer lines can do the trick. DO NOT write more lines than is necessary to get the job done.
- When working on established codebases, be clear and surgical in your code edits. Keep changes as minimal as possible, unless absolutely impossible. Retain as much of the original code as possible.
- Avoid changing existing code if adding a new function helps achieve the objective, unless asked to modify existing behaviour.
- Don't introduce a new package import unless absolutely necessary. And if so, ask before commiting to writing.
- Always assume that there is a `.venv` available and uv is the default package manager. So, use `uv run` when testing.
- Always consider the architecture of the codebase and consider what a good solution is that fits within that. Don't just write patch code that works for the sake of it.
- Check the current version of python in `.venv` or `.python-version`. If not, assume it's 3.10++ and always use dict and list instead of Dict and List from typing. Same with | None instead of Optional.
- If you are testing and running code, please make sure to clean up any unnecessary code by trimming it down to the minimum required to make it run. For example, what if you removed the try block, or the newly added if statement, and the code still runs fine? This means it was unnecessary.
- After a fix works, perform a redundancy sweep on your own changes and remove any branch/fallback that is not required for correctness.
- Respect established invariants and schemas. If the project uses one canonical field name (for example `date`), do not add aliases/fallbacks (for example `timestamp`) unless explicitly requested.
- Every new guard/branch must be justified by a reproduced failing case. If there is no concrete failing case, do not add the guard.
- Keep return contracts single-shape unless explicitly required otherwise. Do not silently support multiple return types when one is the agreed contract.
- Prefer fixing data/model drift at the source over adding compatibility handling downstream.

